/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 46 0 R
/T (list.insert\(i, x\) costs O\(n\) because the n - i items after the position are shifted \(and finding the right place by date with bisect is O\(log n\), but the shift still dominates\). My timing showed about 190 microseconds for 365,000 items versus 0.07 microseconds for append. For one year \(n = 365\) this is only a few hundred element moves, which is negligible, so I would still use a list. Better still, because index = day of the year, pre-fill 365 slots with None and place a late reading with readings[day] = value in O\(1\) with no shifting. If the data became very large with frequent out-of-order insertions, I would switch to a dictionary keyed by date, or a sorted container such as)
/E (list.insert\(i, x\) costs O\(n\) because the n - i items after the position are shifted \(and finding the right place by date with bisect is O\(log n\), but the shift still dominates\). My timing showed about 190 microseconds for 365,000 items versus 0.07 microseconds for append. For one year \(n = 365\) this is only a few hundred element moves, which is negligible, so I would still use a list. Better still, because index = day of the year, pre-fill 365 slots with None and place a late reading with readings[day] = value in O\(1\) with no shifting. If the data became very large with frequent out-of-order insertions, I would switch to a dictionary keyed by date, or a sorted container such as)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [50]
>>
