444 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 50 0 R
/T (The tuple is only 16 bytes smaller here, because both hold one 8-byte pointer per item. A list header is larger \(56 vs 40 bytes\), and a list built by repeated append also over-allocates spare capacity \(roughly 12 percent\), so such a list can be noticeably bigger than the tuple. getsizeof is shallow: it does not count the 1,000,000 int objects themselves \(about 28 bytes each for large ints\), which are the same for both containers.)
/E (The tuple is only 16 bytes smaller here, because both hold one 8-byte pointer per item. A list header is larger \(56 vs 40 bytes\), and a list built by repeated append also over-allocates spare capacity \(roughly 12 percent\), so such a list can be noticeably bigger than the tuple. getsizeof is shallow: it does not count the 1,000,000 int objects themselves \(about 28 bytes each for large ints\), which are the same for both containers.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
445 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 50 0 R
/T (Conclusion: tuples give immutability, hashability \(usable as dictionary keys such as the GPS stops\) and a small speed and memory advantage when created; indexing speed is the same. Use lists for data that changes \(the zone counts\), but create nested lists with a comprehension and choose copy.copy or copy.deepcopy according to whether inner objects are mutable.)
/E (Conclusion: tuples give immutability, hashability \(usable as dictionary keys such as the GPS stops\) and a small speed and memory advantage when created; indexing speed is the same. Use lists for data that changes \(the zone counts\), but create nested lists with a comprehension and choose copy.copy or copy.deepcopy according to whether inner objects are mutable.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
