/Title (A dictionary finds a key through its hash value, which must never change while the key is in the table. A tuple is immutable, so its hash \(computed from its contents\) is constant, and it defines __hash__. A list is mutable, so its contents \(and any hash based on them\) could change after insertion and the entry could never be found again; Python therefore sets list.__hash__ = None and raises TypeError: unhashable type: 'list' \(seen in the output\). Note that a tuple is hashable only if all its items are hashable: \(1, [2]\) cannot be a key.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 637 0 R
>>
endobj
